# Q7 — Session-create permissions evaluated after agent rules?
Date: 2026-10-13. Container agency-spike-q. Session with per-task grants:
  "permissions":[{"action":"edit","resource":"notes.txt","effect":"allow"},
                 {"action":"spikeprobe","resource":"*","effect":"ask"}]

## Commands + trimmed output (real route POST /api/session/{id}/permission)

$ # session S3 created with the grants above: HTTP 200, permissions echoed
$ POST /api/session/S3/permission {"action":"spikeprobe","resources":["probe-1"]}
{"data":{"id":"per_...","effect":"ask"}}        # session ask-rule wins -> pends
$ POST ... {"action":"shell","resources":["echo hi"]}
{"data":{"id":"per_...","effect":"allow"}}      # baseline `shell *` allow stands
$ POST ... {"action":"edit","resources":["notes.txt"]}
{"data":{"id":"per_...","effect":"allow"}}      # POSITIVE: per-task allow over deny-all
$ POST ... {"action":"edit","resources":["other.txt"]}
{"data":{"id":"per_...","effect":"deny"}}       # NEGATIVE: outside the grant refused
$ GET /api/session/S3/permission
{"data":[{...spikeprobe request...}]}           # ask pends and is listed
$ POST /api/session/S3/permission/<rid>/reply {"decision":"reject",...}
HTTP 204
$ GET /api/session/S3/permission
{"data":[]}                                     # rejected request disappears

Source: review/opencode/packages/core/src/permission.ts:158-163,87-97
  configured = merge(agent?.permissions ?? [deny-all], session.permissions)
  evaluate = findLast match; unmatched -> ask.

Race note: a permission check ~1 min after boot on a fresh session location
returned deny for shell, then allow on retry. Agent rules resolve
asynchronously per location; early checks fail CLOSED (deny). The
Dispatcher (Stage 1c) must create-then-use with tolerance for this, never
by weakening the check.

## Observation
Yes. All four matrix cells observed live, plus list/reject round-trip.
Session rules evaluate after (and override) agent rules; the deny-all
baseline holds everywhere else. Model-driven edit allow/refuse rides the
same evaluator; scripts/spike.sh covers it end to end once keyed.

## Verdict: yes (fallback "static globs" not needed)
